From f7928bb1cef300d0e28af653466093e7e5a259e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Knifethrower <andersenpeters@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 09:15:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] audio: stream music and records instead of decoding them
 whole

A stream: true sound (music, records) was decoded into one mono Vec<f32>
on a thread and held for as long as it played. 26.3's median music track
is 53 MiB of samples (creative tracks 64-87 MiB, the_end 152 MiB), and the
Vec grew by doubling, so starting a track briefly took up to twice that.
On a 1 GB handheld at ~430 MiB that is enough for the OOM killer.

Stream them as vanilla does: Channel.attachBufferStream queues
QUEUED_BUFFER_COUNT (4) buffers of BUFFER_DURATION_SECONDS (1) and refills
them as they play. A decoder thread keeps a queue 4 seconds ahead of the
mixer, starts the channel once it is full, and loops by reopening the
file. It returns at the end, or once the channel has let go of the stream.
Whole sounds for the cache are now trimmed to their samples
(shrink_to_fit), which SoundCache counts.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
